<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Burnes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1841">1841</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Burnes was a Scottish officer and explorer in the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. His journey through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 1831–1833, described in his best-selling Travels into Bokhara, made him famous. He was political officer at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a> and was killed by a mob there in November 1841.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-burnes.jpg" alt="Alexander Burnes" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Print made by: Lowes Cato Dickinson After: Sir Vincent Eyre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prison_Sketches._Comprising_portraits_of_the_Cabul_prisoners,_and_other_subjects_(BM_1970,0527.2.2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
